Here the graph stores each course's prerequisites directly. The recursion first finishes those prerequisites and then appends the current course. Two sets separate courses that are already complete from courses on the current recursion path.

The current-path set is important. A course being seen before is not always a problem, but finding it on the active path proves a cycle.

```python
class Solution:
    def findOrder(self, N, arr):
        prerequisites = [[] for _ in range(N)]
        for course, prerequisite in arr:
            prerequisites[course].append(prerequisite)

        finished = set()
        active = set()
        order = []

        def dfs(course):
            if course in active:
                return False
            if course in finished:
                return True

            active.add(course)
            for prerequisite in prerequisites[course]:
                if not dfs(prerequisite):
                    return False

            active.remove(course)
            finished.add(course)
            order.append(course)
            return True

        for course in range(N):
            if not dfs(course):
                return []

        return order
```

A small dry run with `arr = [[1,0], [2,1]]` starts at course `0`, then visits `1`, then `2`. The append order is `[0, 1, 2]`, which already has prerequisites first because the graph points from a course to its prerequisites.

Important cases include `N = 0`, which returns an empty list, and a disconnected graph, where DFS is started for every course so no component is skipped.

Time complexity: `O(N + E)` because each course and edge is visited at most once.
Space complexity: `O(N + E)`.

When several courses are ready at the same time, this version takes the smallest course number first. The heap is not needed for validity, but it makes the returned order predictable.

The graph still points from a prerequisite to the courses it unlocks. A cycle leaves some indegrees above zero, so those courses never reach the heap.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <queue>
#include <functional>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
  public: vector < int > findOrder(int N, vector < vector < int >> arr) {
    vector<vector<int>> graph(N);
    vector<int> indegree(N, 0);

    for (const vector<int>& edge : arr) {
      graph[edge[1]].push_back(edge[0]);
      indegree[edge[0]]++;
    }

    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> ready;
    for (int course = 0; course < N; course++) {
      if (indegree[course] == 0) {
        ready.push(course);
      }
    }

    vector<int> order;
    while (!ready.empty()) {
      int current = ready.top();
      ready.pop();
      order.push_back(current);

      for (int next : graph[current]) {
        indegree[next]--;
        if (indegree[next] == 0) {
          ready.push(next);
        }
      }
    }

    if (order.size() != static_cast<size_t>(N)) {
      return {};
    }
    return order;
  }
};
```

For `[[1,0], [2,0]]`, both `1` and `2` become ready after `0`, and the heap chooses `1` first. Any valid ordering would be accepted, but this gives the smaller available number priority.

Time complexity: `O((N + E) log N)` because each course can enter and leave the heap once.
Space complexity: `O(N + E)`.

This version uses DFS instead of a queue. Each course has one of three states: unvisited, currently being explored, or finished. Seeing a currently explored course means DFS came back to the same path, so there is a cycle.

A course is added to the answer after its dependent courses have been visited. Reversing that list places prerequisites first.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public int[] findOrder(int N, int[][] arr) {
        List<List<Integer>> graph = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            graph.add(new ArrayList<>());
        }

        for (int[] edge : arr) {
            int course = edge[0];
            int prerequisite = edge[1];
            graph.get(course).add(prerequisite);
        }

        int[] state = new int[N];
        List<Integer> reversedOrder = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int course = 0; course < N; course++) {
            if (!visit(course, graph, state, reversedOrder)) {
                return new int[0];
            }
        }

        Collections.reverse(reversedOrder);
        int[] order = new int[N];
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            order[i] = reversedOrder.get(i);
        }
        return order;
    }

    private boolean visit(int course, List<List<Integer>> graph,
                          int[] state, List<Integer> order) {
        if (state[course] == 1) {
            return false;
        }
        if (state[course] == 2) {
            return true;
        }

        state[course] = 1;
        for (int prerequisite : graph.get(course)) {
            if (!visit(prerequisite, graph, state, order)) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        state[course] = 2;
        order.add(course);
        return true;
    }
}
```

The edge case of no prerequisites is simple: every course finishes without finding a cycle. A self-dependency such as `[2,2]` is detected immediately as a cycle.

Time complexity: `O(N + E)`.
Space complexity: `O(N + E)`, including the recursion stack and graph.

A queue works well here. For each pair `[course, prerequisite]`, the edge goes from the prerequisite to the course. The indegree tells how many prerequisites are still missing.

Start with courses whose indegree is zero. After taking one, reduce the indegree of the courses it unlocks. If the result does not contain all courses, a cycle is blocking the schedule.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <queue>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
  public: vector < int > findOrder(int N, vector < vector < int >> arr) {
    vector<vector<int>> graph(N);
    vector<int> indegree(N, 0);

    for (const vector<int>& edge : arr) {
      int course = edge[0];
      int prerequisite = edge[1];
      graph[prerequisite].push_back(course);
      indegree[course]++;
    }

    queue<int> ready;
    for (int course = 0; course < N; course++) {
      if (indegree[course] == 0) {
        ready.push(course);
      }
    }

    vector<int> order;
    while (!ready.empty()) {
      int current = ready.front();
      ready.pop();
      order.push_back(current);

      for (int next : graph[current]) {
        indegree[next]--;
        if (indegree[next] == 0) {
          ready.push(next);
        }
      }
    }

    return order.size() == static_cast<size_t>(N) ? order : vector<int>();
  }
};
```

For example, with `N = 4` and pairs `[[1,0], [2,0], [3,1], [3,2]]`, the queue starts with `0`. It then makes `1` and `2` available, and course `3` becomes available after both are processed.

Time complexity: `O(N + E)`, where `E` is the number of prerequisite pairs.
Space complexity: `O(N + E)` for the graph, queue, indegrees, and result.

This keeps the prerequisite lists instead of building outgoing edges. When a course is completed, the code checks every course and removes that completed course from its prerequisite list.

It is easy to follow, but it does more repeated scanning than the usual adjacency-list version. That is the main trade-off of this implementation.

```python
from collections import deque


class Solution:
    def findOrder(self, N, arr):
        needed = [[] for _ in range(N)]
        for course, prerequisite in arr:
            needed[course].append(prerequisite)

        remaining = [len(needed[course]) for course in range(N)]
        ready = deque()
        for course in range(N):
            if remaining[course] == 0:
                ready.append(course)

        order = []
        while ready:
            completed = ready.popleft()
            order.append(completed)

            for course in range(N):
                if completed in needed[course]:
                    remaining[course] -= 1
                    if remaining[course] == 0:
                        ready.append(course)

        return order if len(order) == N else []
```

A cycle has no course with zero remaining prerequisites. For example, with `[[0,1], [1,0]]`, the queue starts empty and the returned list is empty. With no prerequisite pairs, all courses enter the queue at the start.

This code does not change the input `arr`. The list membership checks make it slower than storing an outgoing adjacency list, but the dependency direction is visible in the code.

Time complexity: `O(N * (N + E))` in the worst case because each processed course scans all courses and their prerequisite lists.
Space complexity: `O(N + E)`.

